    description: "The Bulgar Khan Asparuh leads his steppe people across the Danube in force; Emperor Constantine IV, distracted by the Arab threat, fails to dislodge them and in 681 AD signs a treaty recognising the First Bulgarian Empire on Byzantine territory - the first time the empire has acknowledged a permanent barbarian state within its claimed borders. The Bulgars gradually merge with the existing Slavic population; the state converts to Orthodox Christianity in 864 AD and becomes one of Byzantium's most formidable rivals and cultural heirs. For the next three centuries the Byzantine-Bulgarian relationship - oscillating between war, diplomacy, and cultural exchange - defines the northern frontier of the Greek-Orthodox world. The Bulgarian Empire at its height under Simeon the Great (893–927) nearly replaces Byzantium entirely.",
