    description: "After the Arab conquest of Egypt (641), the Byzantine Empire clings to coastal strongholds across North Africa for decades. In 698 AD an Arab force from the east finally takes Carthage - the ancient city rebuilt under Caesar and Augustus, held by Byzantium since Belisarius expelled the Vandals in 534, now 164 years of Byzantine rule ending in a single campaign. The Arabs deliberately raze the city to prevent it ever again serving as a base for Byzantine reconquest; Tunis is built nearby on a fresh site. The Byzantine Empire permanently loses North Africa. Its western frontier is now effectively Greece itself. With Africa gone and Italy slipping away, Byzantium contracts into the Greek-speaking eastern Mediterranean world it will increasingly define: less Roman inheritance, more Greek Orthodox civilisation.",
