    description: "When Russia goes to war with the Ottoman empire in the Crimea, Greece sees its chance: Greek irregular volunteers flood into Ottoman-held Thessaly and Epirus, backed unofficially by Athens. Britain and France, determined to keep the Ottomans fighting and Greece neutral, land troops and occupy Piraeus in May 1854. They stay for three years. The occupation is profoundly humiliating: it demonstrates that the 'protecting powers' can override Greek sovereignty at will, that the Great Idea cannot be pursued against their wishes, and that Greek foreign policy is constrained by the interests of powers whose agendas frequently conflict with Greek expansion. Otto is forced to dismiss his pro-Russian government. The occupation accelerates the domestic discontent that leads to his deposition in 1862.",
