    description: "The great Cretan revolt of 1866–1869 against Ottoman rule produces its defining moment at the monastery of Arkadi in November 1866. Around 1,000 Cretan fighters and refugees - including many women and children - shelter in the monastery as Ottoman forces of 15,000 under Mustafa Pasha breach the outer walls. Rather than surrender, the abbot Gabriel orders the powder magazine ignited. The explosion kills approximately 900 people - Cretans and Ottomans alike. The surviving Cretans are taken captive; Abbot Gabriel is killed. The event convulses European opinion: Victor Hugo, Garibaldi, and other liberal champions take up the Cretan cause; fundraising committees form across Europe; the revolt receives wide international coverage. The revolt ultimately fails - the Ottomans suppress it by 1869 - but Arkadi becomes the iconic symbol of Cretan sacrifice, directly analogous to Missolonghi in the 1821 generation. It is the seed of the international pressure that eventually produces Cretan autonomy (1898) and union with Greece (1913).",
