    description: "In March 1947 President Truman addresses a joint session of Congress requesting $400 million for Greece and Turkey and declaring that the United States will support free peoples resisting subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressure. The speech is largely drafted in response to the Greek Civil War: Britain has just informed Washington it can no longer afford to sustain its Greek commitment; the communist DSE controls large areas of northern Greece; without outside intervention the Greek government may fall. The Truman Doctrine authorises the US to intervene anywhere in the world where communist expansion threatens - it is the founding document of American Cold War foreign policy. For Greece, it means American military advisors, weapons, and $400 million in aid; it also means Greece enters the Cold War firmly on the Western side, a subordinate partner to American strategic priorities, with all the constraints that entails. The doctrine is partly drafted because of Greece and shapes the world for fifty years.",
