    year: 1948,
    description: "Makronisos - a barren, waterless island off the Attica coast - becomes from 1947 onward the primary detention site for political prisoners: communists, suspected sympathisers, draft evaders deemed politically unreliable, and family members of fighters on the other side. At its peak in 1948–1950 it holds over 40,000 people. The regime's stated purpose is 're-education': prisoners are subjected to starvation, beatings, sleep deprivation, mock executions, and sustained psychological pressure until they sign a 'declaration of repentance' renouncing communism. Those who refuse are sent to harsher conditions or transferred to military courts. The poet Yannis Ritsos - later a Nobel nominee - is imprisoned there; so are hundreds of other intellectuals, artists, and trade unionists. Makronisos is the institutionalised face of the post-Civil War settlement: the systematic suppression that defines Greek political life until the Metapolitefsi of 1974. It cannot be publicly discussed in Greece for decades; the island is declared a protected historical monument only in 1989.",
